1
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:04,829
Service accounts are incredibly powerful and

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:08,350
incredibly dangerous when used incorrectly.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:11,470
They are like the robot users of your cloud environment.

4
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:15,949
They deploy apps, run pipelines, automate infrastructure.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,389
Let's see how we can use it in a proper, secure manner.

6
00:00:20,469 --> 00:00:23,840
In the Google Cloud console at the top, let's select the project.

7
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:27,459
So let's say my project is payment‑app‑service‑prod.

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,120
Inside the project and go inside the IAM,

9
00:00:31,159 --> 00:00:34,479
and inside the IAM, you see Service Accounts.

10
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:36,270
So click on that Service Accounts.

11
00:00:36,439 --> 00:00:40,119
Now here on the top you can see Create service account.

12
00:00:40,279 --> 00:00:41,810
So click on Create service account.

13
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:43,840
Now give a meaningful name.

14
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,080
Because over a period of time you might have a couple of service

15
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:51,840
accounts and then it will be difficult to identify which service account

16
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,400
do what or what was the purpose of its creation.

17
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,500
Now click on Create and continue.

18
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:00,680
Now the console asks which role this service account should have.

19
00:01:00,819 --> 00:01:03,080
This is where the things often go wrong.

20
00:01:03,369 --> 00:01:06,300
People generally go and pick Basic,

21
00:01:06,419 --> 00:01:11,220
and under the Basic they pick Editor because it sounds more convenient,

22
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,779
but we're not doing that because this is not the right way of doing.

23
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:20,529
The right way of doing is to give the least privileges roles.

24
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:21,419
For example,

25
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:27,319
let's say this service account is going to be utilized for CI/CD purpose,

26
00:01:27,519 --> 00:01:31,699
and there I need only the permissions for Cloud Run,

27
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:34,160
Artifact Registry, and maybe for logs.

28
00:01:34,239 --> 00:01:38,370
So there is no point in giving all the entire editor permission.

29
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:42,519
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to choose a specific permission.

30
00:01:42,639 --> 00:01:44,400
I'm using it for deployment,

31
00:01:44,519 --> 00:01:48,322
so I'm just choosing the role as Run admin. Add

32
00:01:48,322 --> 00:01:53,492
another role for Artifact Registry, Artifact Registry Reader. And similarly,

33
00:01:53,602 --> 00:01:57,402
I need just one role for log, so I'm just saying that

34
00:01:57,402 --> 00:02:00,093
Cloud Build Logging Service Agent, and that's it.

35
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:04,972
So I don't need to give all the permission to this specific service account.

36
00:02:05,203 --> 00:02:08,560
I can give a very specific permission which is needed for

37
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:10,966
this role. And now I'm saying Done.

38
00:02:11,126 --> 00:02:15,425
We should also avoid using these service account keys because

39
00:02:15,725 --> 00:02:18,606
Google's security guidance is very crystal clear.

40
00:02:18,686 --> 00:02:20,565
Avoid using service account keys.

41
00:02:20,695 --> 00:02:24,725
Instead, use workload identity federation or

42
00:02:24,725 --> 00:02:27,246
workload identity wherever possible.

43
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:32,886
The problem is service account keys are dangerous because they don't expire.

44
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:34,509
They can be copied.

45
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:36,729
They can be leaked on GitHub.

46
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,339
They give attackers long‑lived access, and rotating them is very painful.

47
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:45,240
So that's why whenever you're going to use the service account,

48
00:02:45,479 --> 00:02:47,800
use it with the workload identity.

49
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:51,830
If at some point you want to delete this specific service account,

50
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:56,279
you can do it simply by selecting here and say Delete,

51
00:02:56,520 --> 00:03:00,320
and this will delete the service account which we have created.
